        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.083,
            0.743,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are many examples in our daily life, where we can observe conduction, convection, and radiation happening together."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.141,
            0.635,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider the case of water being heated up, as shown in Fig. 7.8. Let us "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "identify",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the various ways in which the pan and the water get heated up, as well as the warmth we feel around the flame and the hot pan. Heat is transferred from the flame to the utensil by conduction. Subsequently, water in the utensil gets heated up by convection. The warmth that we feel around the flame and the hot utensil is due to radiation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.644,
            0.133,
            0.928,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.644,
                0.133,
                0.928,
                0.26
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.645,
                0.264,
                0.897,
                0.281
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.8: Heating water in a pan"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.31,
            0.274,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us wrap up!"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.33,
            0.743,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the activities, examples, and discussions so far, we find that there are three processes by which heat gets transferred. These are conduction, convection, and radiation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.388,
            0.743,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In conduction, heating takes place when one particle receives heat, transfers heat to the next particle in contact, and so on. The particles themselves do not move away from their positions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.464,
            0.742,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In convection, heat transfer takes place by the actual movement of particles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.503,
            0.743,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that in conduction and convection, a medium must be present whose particles help in the transfer of heat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.542,
            0.743,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the case of radiation, heat travels from one place to another and no material medium is required for its transfer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.612,
            0.405,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "FASCINATING FACTS"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
